1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,940
Imagine an opponent smashing deep into your
corner, and instead of panicking,

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:05,561
you smile.

3
00:00:05,911 --> 00:00:08,719
You step sideways, let the ball pass you,

4
00:00:08,719 --> 00:00:12,930
and watch the glass slow that violent
smash into an easy reset.

5
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,968
Most amateurs treat the
walls like a hazard,

6
00:00:15,968 --> 00:00:19,552
scrambling frantically and getting
pinned against the glass.

7
00:00:19,902 --> 00:00:24,920
Today, I will show you the physics and
footwork rules of all four wall rebounds,

8
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:28,265
turning the glass into your
ultimate defensive weapon.

9
00:00:28,615 --> 00:00:33,775
In padel, the perimeter walls are not
out-of-bounds boundaries; they are active

10
00:00:33,775 --> 00:00:36,807
playing surfaces governed by
collision physics.

11
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:41,658
Every rebound you will face belongs to
one of four physical families.

12
00:00:42,008 --> 00:00:47,085
First is the Single Back Glass, where deep
balls rebound forward into the court.

13
00:00:47,435 --> 00:00:52,875
Second is the Side Glass, where oblique angles
compress your lateral hitting space.

14
00:00:53,225 --> 00:00:58,767
Third is the Double Glass corner, where the ball
strikes two walls and either opens

15
00:00:58,767 --> 00:01:01,076
into court or traps into your body.

16
00:01:01,426 --> 00:01:06,851
And fourth is the Metallic Wire Mesh, where
compliant metal grids create dead,

17
00:01:06,851 --> 00:01:08,637
unpredictable deflections.

18
00:01:08,987 --> 00:01:13,744
Master these four families, and you will
never get jammed off the walls again.

19
00:01:14,094 --> 00:01:18,382
The single back glass is the
foundation of all padel defense.

20
00:01:18,732 --> 00:01:23,149
When a ball crosses the service line,
three meters from the back wall,

21
00:01:23,149 --> 00:01:26,135
amateurs panic and short-hop
it off their shoes.

22
00:01:26,485 --> 00:01:32,165
Tempered glass follows specular reflection: the
angle of reflection equals the angle

23
00:01:32,165 --> 00:01:33,034
of incidence.

24
00:01:33,384 --> 00:01:38,555
An eighty kilometer per hour smash loses
thirty-five percent of its speed on the

25
00:01:38,555 --> 00:01:41,683
turf bounce and twenty-five
percent on the glass.

26
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:46,100
It floats forward at thirty to
forty kilometers per hour,

27
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:51,569
buying you zero point four to zero point
seven seconds of extra reaction time.

28
00:01:51,919 --> 00:01:56,869
Think of throwing a rubber ball against a brick
wall: stand against the brick and it

29
00:01:56,869 --> 00:02:01,469
hits your knuckles, but take two steps back
and it drops gently into your open

30
00:02:01,469 --> 00:02:01,818
hands.

31
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:05,968
Execute a sideways unit turn as the
ball crosses the net,

32
00:02:05,968 --> 00:02:09,506
turning your shoulders
perpendicular to the back wall.

33
00:02:09,856 --> 00:02:14,528
Track backward, stop one to one-and-a-half
meters in front of the glass,

34
00:02:14,528 --> 00:02:16,896
and let the ball pass your body line.

35
00:02:17,246 --> 00:02:22,207
As it rebounds forward, transfer your weight and
strike thirty to fifty centimeters

36
00:02:22,207 --> 00:02:23,625
ahead of your front hip.

37
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:29,702
Train this with the Self-Drop Glass Feed: stand
one-and-a-half meters from the glass,

38
00:02:29,702 --> 00:02:34,364
drop the ball into the wall, and drive
the rebound into a target cone.

39
00:02:34,714 --> 00:02:39,578
Do four sets of twelve reps twice a week on
both forehand and backhand sides.

40
00:02:39,928 --> 00:02:45,178
The biggest mistake is hugging the glass:
standing closer than one meter suffocates

41
00:02:45,178 --> 00:02:47,053
your arm and ruins your swing.

42
00:02:47,403 --> 00:02:52,757
The side glass introduces oblique angles that
violently compress your lateral

43
00:02:52,757 --> 00:02:53,718
hitting space.

44
00:02:54,068 --> 00:02:58,947
Side glass shots strike at acute angles
between thirty and sixty degrees,

45
00:02:58,947 --> 00:03:01,321
sliding fast across the court width.

46
00:03:01,671 --> 00:03:04,505
Amateurs chase the ball
toward the side wall,

47
00:03:04,505 --> 00:03:07,708
getting their arm pinned
directly against the glass.

48
00:03:08,059 --> 00:03:12,652
Think of a highway exit ramp: steer into
the guardrail and you crash,

49
00:03:12,652 --> 00:03:17,574
but stay centered in your lane and you
follow the curve with total control.

50
00:03:17,923 --> 00:03:23,070
Maintain an active one-meter buffer from the
side wall and track the ball with a

51
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:23,832
stable gaze.

52
00:03:24,183 --> 00:03:28,944
Stay toward the center, let the ball strike
the side glass past your hip,

53
00:03:28,944 --> 00:03:32,226
and step into the open court as
it rebounds across.

54
00:03:32,575 --> 00:03:37,869
Hit early to drive down the line, or let it
travel twenty centimeters into center

55
00:03:37,869 --> 00:03:39,871
court to open a crosscourt lob.

56
00:03:40,221 --> 00:03:45,678
Train this with the Side Wall Spacing Drill:
place three cones one-point-two meters

57
00:03:45,678 --> 00:03:47,432
parallel to the side glass.

58
00:03:47,782 --> 00:03:51,801
Feed angled balls into the glass,
stay inside the cones,

59
00:03:51,801 --> 00:03:54,481
and drive controlled crosscourt balls.

60
00:03:54,831 --> 00:03:58,372
Perform four sets of ten reps
per side twice a week.

61
00:03:58,722 --> 00:04:04,397
The double glass corner is where amateur players
experience total sensory overload.

62
00:04:04,747 --> 00:04:08,536
In the ninety-degree corner between
the back and side glass,

63
00:04:08,536 --> 00:04:11,083
the ball strikes two walls consecutively.

64
00:04:11,433 --> 00:04:17,142
Coaching science divides double glass into two
distinct realities: Doble Pared Que

65
00:04:17,142 --> 00:04:19,412
Abre, and Doble Pared Que Cierra.

66
00:04:19,761 --> 00:04:25,816
Doble Pared Que Abre means the double wall opens:
the ball hits the side glass first,

67
00:04:25,816 --> 00:04:30,534
then the back glass second, rebounding
outward toward center court.

68
00:04:30,885 --> 00:04:34,810
This is the only double wall where
you must rotate your body.

69
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:40,292
Execute a smooth one-hundred-and-eighty-degree
pivot on the balls of your feet,

70
00:04:40,292 --> 00:04:44,461
uncoiling your shoulders so your feet
face the net as you strike.

71
00:04:44,811 --> 00:04:48,445
Think of a figure skater: rotate
with the ball's orbit,

72
00:04:48,445 --> 00:04:52,598
and you emerge facing forward right as
the ball presents itself.

73
00:04:52,948 --> 00:04:56,831
Now look at Doble Pared Que Cierra:
the double wall closes.

74
00:04:57,181 --> 00:05:01,179
The ball hits the back glass first,
then the side glass second,

75
00:05:01,179 --> 00:05:03,240
trapping inward toward your body.

76
00:05:03,589 --> 00:05:06,073
If you spin on a closing double wall,

77
00:05:06,073 --> 00:05:10,126
you turn your back to the net while
the ball dies behind you,

78
00:05:10,126 --> 00:05:13,786
increasing unforced errors by over
four hundred percent.

79
00:05:14,137 --> 00:05:16,740
On a closing double wall, do not spin.

80
00:05:17,089 --> 00:05:21,169
Stay sideways, step forward into the
court away from the corner,

81
00:05:21,169 --> 00:05:25,814
and let the ball bounce off the side glass
directly into your strike zone.

82
00:05:26,165 --> 00:05:29,998
Think of a dog in a cramped hallway:
spinning makes you dizzy,

83
00:05:29,998 --> 00:05:34,378
but stepping into the open living room
lets the dog run straight to you.

84
00:05:34,728 --> 00:05:40,599
Practice the Abre versus Cierra Recognition
Drill: have a partner feed random corner

85
00:05:40,599 --> 00:05:44,328
balls, calling Abre or Cierra
before the first impact.

86
00:05:44,678 --> 00:05:47,161
Do four sets of twelve random feeds,

87
00:05:47,161 --> 00:05:51,121
targeting at least ten correct
footwork executions per set.

88
00:05:51,471 --> 00:05:56,416
Beginners should first practice slow shadow
footwork on the balls of your feet

89
00:05:56,416 --> 00:06:01,361
before full-speed ball drills, wearing
herringbone turf shoes to prevent ankle

90
00:06:01,361 --> 00:06:01,924
rollover.

91
00:06:02,274 --> 00:06:07,921
Safety caution: always unweight your feet with a
light hop before pivoting; spinning

92
00:06:07,921 --> 00:06:13,436
with flat heels on sand turf creates violent
twisting torque on knee ligaments and

93
00:06:13,436 --> 00:06:13,901
ankles.

94
00:06:14,251 --> 00:06:17,026
The fourth family is the
metallic wire mesh,

95
00:06:17,026 --> 00:06:20,480
covering the side walls and upper
sections of the court.

96
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:24,130
While tempered glass is rigid
and predictable,

97
00:06:24,130 --> 00:06:29,817
the wire mesh is composed of electro-welded
metal wires forming fifty-millimeter

98
00:06:29,817 --> 00:06:30,238
grids.

99
00:06:30,588 --> 00:06:35,448
When a padel ball strikes the mesh, the
metal wires flex and vibrate,

100
00:06:35,448 --> 00:06:37,948
absorbing tremendous kinetic energy.

101
00:06:38,298 --> 00:06:44,083
The coefficient of restitution drops from zero
point seven-five on glass down to

102
00:06:44,083 --> 00:06:49,583
zero point three-five on mesh, causing the
ball to drop dead toward the turf.

103
00:06:49,933 --> 00:06:52,728
If the ball hits a welded wire junction,

104
00:06:52,728 --> 00:06:58,317
it deflects upward or sideways at erratic
angles of fifteen to forty-five degrees.

105
00:06:58,667 --> 00:07:01,931
Playing off the mesh requires
an ultra-low crouch,

106
00:07:01,931 --> 00:07:05,707
flexing your knees so your racket
head sits at shin height.

107
00:07:06,057 --> 00:07:09,529
Eliminate your backswing, hold the
paddle out in front,

108
00:07:09,529 --> 00:07:14,674
and use a short blocking punch under thirty
centimeters to lift the dead ball back

109
00:07:14,674 --> 00:07:15,294
into play.

110
00:07:15,645 --> 00:07:20,627
Think of billiards versus a chain-link
fence: glass rolls true like slate,

111
00:07:20,627 --> 00:07:25,608
but wire mesh wobbles, absorbs the punch,
and drops the ball straight down.

112
00:07:25,957 --> 00:07:30,857
Train this with the Mesh Drop and React
Drill: wearing shatterproof protective

113
00:07:30,857 --> 00:07:35,943
eyewear, have a partner hit balls into the side
wire fence while you react with a

114
00:07:35,943 --> 00:07:37,370
compact blocking scoop.

115
00:07:37,721 --> 00:07:40,515
Start with two sets of six to eight feeds,

116
00:07:40,515 --> 00:07:43,894
progressing to four sets of ten
as your legs adapt,

117
00:07:43,894 --> 00:07:47,598
and avoid prolonged deep squatting
if you have knee pain.

118
00:07:47,948 --> 00:07:53,324
Never try to blast a winner off the mesh; your
only goal is a safe, soft recovery.

119
00:07:53,674 --> 00:07:56,126
Before taking this blueprint to the court,

120
00:07:56,126 --> 00:07:59,605
there are three critical safety rules
every player must know.

121
00:07:59,955 --> 00:08:05,721
First, protect your eyes: an official padel ball
measures six-point-five centimeters

122
00:08:05,721 --> 00:08:08,638
in diameter, matching the
adult eye socket.

123
00:08:08,987 --> 00:08:14,543
Erratic deflections off the mesh can rocket
toward your face with under zero point

124
00:08:14,543 --> 00:08:16,283
two-five seconds to react.

125
00:08:16,633 --> 00:08:22,554
A landmark hospital registry study in Acta
Ophthalmologica revealed that padel has

126
00:08:22,554 --> 00:08:27,191
become the leading cause of
sports-related eye trauma in Sweden,

127
00:08:27,191 --> 00:08:30,116
causing severe hyphema and retinal tears.

128
00:08:30,466 --> 00:08:35,331
Sports ophthalmologists strongly recommend
wearing certified shatterproof

129
00:08:35,331 --> 00:08:39,341
polycarbonate sports eyewear during
close-quarters wall play.

130
00:08:39,691 --> 00:08:44,841
Second, here is a red flag warning: never
back-pedal straight backward blind toward

131
00:08:44,841 --> 00:08:46,987
the glass with your heels dragging.

132
00:08:47,337 --> 00:08:52,011
Blind back-pedaling causes catastrophic
backward falls, concussions,

133
00:08:52,011 --> 00:08:55,060
and violent collisions with
the glass panels.

134
00:08:55,409 --> 00:09:00,957
Always retreat using sideways crossover steps
while keeping your eyes on both the

135
00:09:00,957 --> 00:09:02,513
ball and the wall line.

136
00:09:02,863 --> 00:09:08,297
Third, watch your ankles: sand buildup near the
corner walls causes sudden slipping

137
00:09:08,297 --> 00:09:12,955
or jamming; if you roll an ankle and
cannot bear weight for four steps,

138
00:09:12,955 --> 00:09:16,836
see a healthcare professional
immediately for an evaluation.

139
00:09:17,187 --> 00:09:20,182
To pull all four rebound
families together,

140
00:09:20,182 --> 00:09:25,016
execute this simple three-step mental
decision flow on every deep shot.

141
00:09:25,366 --> 00:09:29,052
Step one: Read depth—if the ball
crosses the service line,

142
00:09:29,052 --> 00:09:32,363
make the mental commitment to let
it go to the glass.

143
00:09:32,713 --> 00:09:36,268
Step two: Read wall order—if it
hits side then back,

144
00:09:36,268 --> 00:09:41,432
shout Abre and pivot; if it hits back then
side, shout Cierra, step forward,

145
00:09:41,432 --> 00:09:42,505
and do not spin.

146
00:09:42,855 --> 00:09:48,630
And step three: Establish spacing—remember the
golden sequence: Wall, Player, Ball.

147
00:09:48,630 --> 00:09:52,412
Never let the ball get between
your body and the glass.

148
00:09:52,763 --> 00:09:55,067
Never fear the walls in padel again.

149
00:09:55,417 --> 00:10:00,579
Trust the glass deceleration, master your
Abre pivot, step forward on Cierra,

150
00:10:00,579 --> 00:10:02,564
and drop low on the wire mesh.

151
00:10:02,913 --> 00:10:08,128
The court perimeter is not a cage that traps
you; it is your ultimate defensive

152
00:10:08,128 --> 00:10:08,584
reset.

153
00:10:08,584 --> 00:10:09,953
See you on the court.
